WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.180
How many multiplications does it take to compute 2 to the power 13?

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:10.189
The obvious answer is about 13, one for each 2.

00:00:10.189 --> 00:00:12.617
But you can do it with far fewer.

00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:16.370
The trick is to choose a different smaller problem.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:20.127
Welcome back to Lecture 6, Recursion.

00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:23.887
This is part 3: powers, and multiple recursion.

00:00:24.237 --> 00:00:26.504
We'll compute powers in two ways,

00:00:26.504 --> 00:00:30.144
draw a ruler with a function that calls itself twice,

00:00:30.144 --> 00:00:34.197
and then ask when to use recursion, and when to use a loop.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:41.527
Raising x to the power n means multiplying x by itself, n times.

00:00:41.877 --> 00:00:45.686
Recursively: x to the power 0 is 1.

00:00:45.686 --> 00:00:51.997
And x to the power n is x, times x to the power n minus 1.

00:00:52.597 --> 00:00:56.015
In Python: if n is 0, return 1.

00:00:56.015 --> 00:01:01.637
Otherwise, return x times power of x and n minus 1.

00:01:01.987 --> 00:01:06.387
Power of 2 and 10 gives 1024.

00:01:06.737 --> 00:01:08.730
But look at the calls.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.985
Power with exponent n makes n + 1 calls, all in one chain.

00:01:13.985 --> 00:01:17.337
So the stack grows n + 1 frames deep.

00:01:17.687 --> 00:01:21.913
Ask for 2 to the power 2000, and the stack gets too deep.

00:01:21.913 --> 00:01:23.767
We get a recursion error.

00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:27.771
So let's change the smaller problem.

00:01:27.771 --> 00:01:34.447
Instead of going from n to n minus 1, go from n to half of n, rounded down.

00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:42.486
If n is even, x to the power n is x to the power half of n, squared.

00:01:42.486 --> 00:01:46.557
If n is odd, multiply by one more x.

00:01:47.157 --> 00:01:53.576
For example, 2 to the 13 is 2, times 2 to the 6, squared.

00:01:53.576 --> 00:01:57.517
And 2 to the 6 is 2 cubed, squared.

00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:03.452
In the code, the smaller answer is computed once, and stored in `partial`.

00:02:03.452 --> 00:02:06.698
Then `result` is `partial` times `partial`.

00:02:06.698 --> 00:02:10.547
If n is odd, `result` is multiplied by x once more.

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:14.676
Fast power of 2 and 13 gives 8192.

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:19.122
And fast power of 2 and 2000 works fine.

00:02:19.122 --> 00:02:22.457
It equals 2 to the power 2000.

00:02:23.234 --> 00:02:25.740
Pause the video and predict.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.035
How many calls does fast power of 2 and 13 make?

00:02:30.035 --> 00:02:31.914
List the values of n.

00:02:32.464 --> 00:02:39.264
13, 6, 3, 1, 0. Five calls.

00:02:39.614 --> 00:02:46.454
And for 2000, the chain is just 12 calls long, instead of 2001.

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:48.141
One warning.

00:02:48.141 --> 00:02:54.714
You might be tempted to skip the variable, and call fast power twice, on the same half.

00:02:55.064 --> 00:02:56.682
It gives the right answer.

00:02:56.682 --> 00:03:00.664
But now each call makes two recursive calls on the same problem,

00:03:00.664 --> 00:03:04.024
and the total work is back to about n multiplications.

00:03:04.374 --> 00:03:10.214
Compute the smaller answer once, store it in a variable, and reuse it.

00:03:11.001 --> 00:03:14.950
So far, each call has made at most one recursive call.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:18.388
Now let's meet functions that make two or more.

00:03:18.388 --> 00:03:20.801
That's called multiple recursion.

00:03:21.151 --> 00:03:23.313
Our example is an English ruler.

00:03:23.313 --> 00:03:28.111
It has a long tick at each whole inch, with the inch number next to it.

00:03:28.461 --> 00:03:31.581
Between 2 inches is a tick at the half inch.

00:03:31.581 --> 00:03:35.623
Between those are ticks at the quarter inches, and so on.

00:03:35.623 --> 00:03:37.821
Each level is one dash shorter.

00:03:38.171 --> 00:03:40.987
Look at the part between two whole inches.

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:42.931
Its center tick has length 3.

00:03:43.281 --> 00:03:49.753
Above the center is a smaller copy of the same pattern, with a center tick of length 2.

00:03:49.753 --> 00:03:51.761
And below it, another copy.

00:03:52.111 --> 00:03:55.704
So an interval is made of three pieces.

00:03:55.704 --> 00:04:01.231
First, a smaller interval, whose center is one dash shorter.

00:04:01.231 --> 00:04:03.258
Next, the center tick.

00:04:03.258 --> 00:04:06.113
Last, another smaller interval.

00:04:06.113 --> 00:04:10.351
And an interval with center length 0 is empty.

00:04:11.134 --> 00:04:12.198
Here's the code.

00:04:12.198 --> 00:04:16.254
`draw_line` prints one tick, with an optional label after it.

00:04:16.604 --> 00:04:18.983
`draw_interval` is the recursive part.

00:04:18.983 --> 00:04:22.990
If the center length is greater than 0, it draws the upper half.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:24.869
Then it draws the center tick.

00:04:24.869 --> 00:04:26.684
Then it draws the lower half.

00:04:27.034 --> 00:04:30.375
That's two recursive calls in one function.

00:04:30.375 --> 00:04:37.754
And the base case is quiet: when the length is 0, the `if` test is false, and nothing is drawn.

00:04:38.354 --> 00:04:43.874
`draw_interval` of 2 prints one dash, two dashes, and one dash.

00:04:44.224 --> 00:04:49.144
And `draw_ruler` puts one interval between each pair of inch ticks.

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:53.854
Let's draw the recursion tree of `draw_interval` of 3.

00:04:54.204 --> 00:04:57.110
It calls `draw_interval` of 2, twice.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.880
Each of those calls `draw_interval` of 1, twice.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.964
And each of those calls `draw_interval` of 0, twice.

00:05:05.314 --> 00:05:10.964
One call at the top, then 2, then 4, then 8 at the bottom.

00:05:10.964 --> 00:05:13.594
15 calls, to print 7 ticks.

00:05:13.944 --> 00:05:19.264
Every call has two calls below it, so this tree is a binary tree.

00:05:19.614 --> 00:05:27.004
Add one more dash, and `draw_interval` of 4 prints 15 ticks, with 31 calls.

00:05:27.004 --> 00:05:30.255
Each extra dash doubles the work.

00:05:30.255 --> 00:05:34.294
Multiple recursion can grow very quickly.

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:38.199
Multiple recursion can also add up a list.

00:05:38.199 --> 00:05:44.388
Split the list into two halves, sum each half recursively, and add the two results.

00:05:44.738 --> 00:05:51.287
The function works on the part of the list from index `start`, up to but not including `stop`.

00:05:51.287 --> 00:05:55.538
Passing two indices means no part of the list is ever copied.

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:57.833
No elements: return 0.

00:05:57.833 --> 00:05:59.867
One element: return it.

00:05:59.867 --> 00:06:05.528
Two or more: split at `mid`, and add the sums of the two halves.

00:06:05.878 --> 00:06:09.798
For the numbers 1 to 7, `binary_sum` returns 28.

00:06:10.148 --> 00:06:11.463
Here's the tree.

00:06:11.463 --> 00:06:14.587
1 to 7 splits into 1 to 3, and 4 to 7.

00:06:14.587 --> 00:06:19.108
Those split again, until each call has a single number.

00:06:19.458 --> 00:06:23.349
On the way back up, the sums combine.

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.138
6 and 22 make 28.

00:06:25.935 --> 00:06:30.615
How does `binary_sum` compare with `linear_sum`, from part 2?

00:06:30.965 --> 00:06:35.174
For a list of n numbers, `linear_sum` makes n + 1 calls,

00:06:35.174 --> 00:06:37.429
and `binary_sum` makes 2n - 1.

00:06:37.429 --> 00:06:39.685
About the same amount of work.

00:06:40.035 --> 00:06:42.485
But the shape is different.

00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:46.025
`linear_sum` is a chain, n frames deep.

00:06:46.025 --> 00:06:50.835
`binary_sum` is a tree, only about log n levels deep.

00:06:51.435 --> 00:06:56.215
On a list of a million numbers, `linear_sum` raises a recursion error.

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:59.835
`binary_sum` needs a stack only about 21 frames deep.

00:07:00.185 --> 00:07:06.441
So: linear recursion makes at most one call per call, and the calls form a chain.

00:07:06.441 --> 00:07:11.385
Multiple recursion makes two or more, and the calls form a tree.

00:07:12.168 --> 00:07:15.568
Last question: recursion, or a loop?

00:07:15.918 --> 00:07:20.614
Every linear recursion in this lecture could also be written with a loop.

00:07:20.614 --> 00:07:22.866
Here's factorial with a `for` loop.

00:07:22.866 --> 00:07:25.118
It gives the same answer as `fact`.

00:07:25.468 --> 00:07:31.548
In Python, a loop is usually the better choice for a simple chain like this.

00:07:31.548 --> 00:07:35.548
It uses no extra frames, so it has no depth limit.

00:07:36.148 --> 00:07:40.985
Some languages turn certain recursive calls into loops automatically.

00:07:40.985 --> 00:07:42.948
Python deliberately doesn't.

00:07:43.298 --> 00:07:48.593
Recursion is the better choice when the problem branches, like the ruler.

00:07:48.593 --> 00:07:54.178
Writing `draw_interval` with loops is possible, but much harder to get right.

00:07:54.778 --> 00:07:57.773
You'll meet many branching problems later:

00:07:57.773 --> 00:08:02.409
folders inside folders, sorting by splitting a list in Lecture 9,

00:08:02.409 --> 00:08:05.618
trees in Lecture 10, and the moves of a game.

00:08:06.401 --> 00:08:08.361
Let's review what we've learned.

00:08:08.711 --> 00:08:12.108
The choice of smaller problem matters.

00:08:12.108 --> 00:08:17.471
When we cut the exponent in half, 2001 calls become just 12.

00:08:17.821 --> 00:08:22.907
Multiple recursion makes two or more calls, so its calls form a tree,

00:08:22.907 --> 00:08:25.781
and the work can double at every level.

00:08:26.131 --> 00:08:28.377
Use a loop for a simple chain.

00:08:28.377 --> 00:08:31.371
Use recursion when the problem branches.

00:08:31.721 --> 00:08:34.914
Here's one to try: the Towers of Hanoi.

00:08:34.914 --> 00:08:40.317
Move a stack of discs from one peg to another, one disc at a time,

00:08:40.317 --> 00:08:44.000
never putting a larger disc on a smaller one.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.321
Hint: first move all the discs except the largest out of the way.

00:08:49.871 --> 00:08:51.694
That's the end of Lecture 6.

00:08:51.694 --> 00:08:56.511
Next time, we start object-oriented programming, with objects and classes.

